{"request_id":"3179a0420d39f2739a865c6c","rewritten_text":"“Keeping firmly before us the particulars to which I have directed your notice—the singular voice, the extraordinary agility, and the astonishing want of motive for a murder of such exceptional atrocity—let us consider the slaughter itself. A woman has been strangled by the strength of hands and forced head foremost up a chimney. Common murderers do not kill in this fashion; still less do they dispose of their victims so. You must admit that this thrusting of the corpse into the chimney has something excessively strange about it, something wholly at variance with our ordinary conception of human conduct, even if we imagine the persons concerned to be the most abandoned of men. Reflect also upon the strength required to drive a body up that opening with such force that several people together could scarcely pull it down.

“Consider next the other signs of a most extraordinary exertion of strength. Upon the hearth lay thick locks—exceedingly thick locks—of grey human hair, torn out by the roots. You know the considerable force required to wrench even twenty or thirty hairs at once from the head. You examined those locks as I did. Fragments of the scalp still clung to their roots—a dreadful sight, and certain evidence of the enormous power employed in tearing out perhaps half a million hairs together. The elderly woman’s throat had not simply been cut: her head had been completely separated from her body, and the weapon was nothing more than a razor. Notice, too, the brutal savagery of these acts. I pass over the bruises on Madame L’Espanaye’s body. Monsieur Dumas and his estimable colleague Monsieur Etienne have declared them to be the work of a blunt instrument, and in this they are entirely right. That instrument was plainly the stone paving of the courtyard, upon which the victim fell from the window overlooking the bed. Simple as this explanation may now appear, the police missed it for the very reason that they overlooked the width of the shutters: the matter of the nails had completely closed their minds to the possibility that the windows could ever have been opened.

“If, together with all this, you have duly considered the chamber’s peculiar disorder, we have now brought into conjunction these ideas: astonishing agility, strength beyond the human, savage ferocity, slaughter without motive, a grotesqueness of horror wholly foreign to humanity, and a voice whose tone was unfamiliar to listeners of many nations, and which contained no distinct or intelligible articulation of syllables.”"}

{"request_id":"e1d2fc991075b096ad03c51f","rewritten_text":"It was after two when a sudden turn brought me close to the lake, near a cove where a little rowing boat lay moored. Our house was not far away, and I could see my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat and, being thoroughly accustomed to such adventures, pushed off from the shore and put all my strength into rowing swiftly across. My father has often told me that, approaching in my white dress with only my tartan rachan wrapped around me, my hair streaming over my shoulders and the boat flying faster than anyone could have thought I could propel it, I looked more like a spirit than a human girl. As I reached the shore, my father steadied the boat; I sprang lightly out and a moment later was in his arms.

Now my life truly began. Everything around me changed from a dull sameness into a brilliant scene of joy and delight. The happiness of being with my father went far beyond even my most hopeful expectations. We were constantly together, and never ran out of things to discuss. He had spent his sixteen years away among peoples almost unknown in Europe. He had traveled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, entering the homes of the inhabitants with a freedom granted to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, his stories, and his descriptions of the countryside filled delightful hours whenever we grew tired of discussing our own plans for the future.

Affection was so unfamiliar to me that I listened in delight to every word when he described his feelings for me during those long years of seeming neglect. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl. But later, as my grief diminished and hope returned, you were the one person my thoughts could turn to. Among cities and deserts, your little fairy figure, as I imagined it, was always drifting before my eyes. The northern breeze felt sweeter and gentler as it refreshed me, because it seemed to bring something of your spirit with it. I often thought of returning at once and taking you away with me to some fertile island where we could live in peace forever. On my journey home, so many fears broke in upon my passionate hopes that my impatience became almost unbearable. I did not dare imagine that the sun might shine and the moon rise over your grave instead of your living form. But no, that has not happened. I have my Mathilda, my comfort and my hope.”"}

{"request_id":"8fdf178d0fba641be1e34035","rewritten_text":"Few could claim a heart so unblemished, or such true humility of spirit united with a steadfast trust in her own honour and a faith in the honour of others. She had led a secluded existence from her earliest years. Her mother had died while she was very young, but her father had given himself entirely to overseeing her education. He held many singular opinions, and these shaped the plan he followed in bringing her up. She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those English heroes who had flourished some centuries earlier, yet knew almost nothing of the events unfolding around her. Few of the authors she had read had written within the preceding fifty years, though apart from this limitation her reading was exceedingly wide. Thus, while she seemed less familiar than he with the secrets of society and life, her knowledge went deeper and rested upon sounder ground; and even had her loveliness and gentleness failed to enchant him, her mind would always have kept his captive. He regarded her as his guide, and adored her so completely that he took pleasure in heightening, in his own thoughts, the feeling of inferiority she sometimes inspired.

His mother died when he was nineteen. At this loss he departed from college, and, setting aside his former companions for a time, withdrew to the neighbourhood where his Diana lived. All his comfort came from her gentle voice and her still dearer caresses. This brief absence from his friends gave him the resolution to declare his independence. He felt that, however much they might mock his proposed marriage, they would not venture to show their scorn once it was accomplished. He therefore sought his guardian's permission, which he gained with some trouble, and that of his beloved's father, which was granted more readily. Without informing anyone else of his design, he had made Diana his wife by the time he reached his twentieth birthday.

He loved her ardently, and her tenderness exercised a charm over him that left no room for thoughts of anyone else. He asked some college friends to visit, but their shallowness revolted him. Diana had rent the veil that had hitherto confined him to boyhood: now he was a man. He wondered that he could ever have shared the stock expressions and opinions of his fellow students, or for an instant have dreaded the disapproval of such people. He relinquished those earlier friendships, not through inconstancy, but because they were truly beneath him."}

{"request_id":"4ba7dfeef32b2d28ef3bc4e5","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a light breeze soon blew it into flame. Noticing this, I made a fan from branches and used it to revive the embers whenever they nearly went out. When night returned, I was pleased to discover that the fire gave light as well as warmth. I also learned that it could improve my food. Some scraps the travelers had left behind were roasted, and these tasted much better than the berries I gathered from the trees. So I tried cooking my food the same way, putting it on the glowing embers. This ruined the berries but greatly improved the nuts and roots.

“Food grew scarce, however, and I often spent an entire day searching without success for a few acorns to ease my hunger. Because of this, I decided to leave the place where I had been living and look for somewhere I could meet my few needs more easily. I deeply regretted having to leave the fire behind. I had found it by accident and did not know how to make another. I spent several hours thinking hard about this problem, but had to give up trying to solve it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I set off through the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days before finally reaching open country. Heavy snow had fallen the night before, leaving the fields entirely white. The scene was bleak, and the cold, wet substance covering the ground chilled my feet.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I desperately wanted food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on a rise in the ground, no doubt built for a shepherd’s use. I had never seen anything like it and examined the building with great curiosity. Its door was open, so I went inside. An old man sat near a fire, preparing his breakfast over it. Hearing a noise, he turned around. When he saw me, he screamed, fled the hut, and ran across the fields at a speed his frail body hardly seemed capable of. His appearance was unlike anyone’s I had seen before, and both it and his flight surprised me somewhat. But the sight of the hut delighted me. Here the snow and rain could not reach me, and the ground was dry. At that moment it seemed as wonderful and heavenly a refuge as Pandemonium had seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

{"request_id":"2179abaf80810ca5f85af46c","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I had no worries about her. She was what people call a capable woman and could manage her affairs without my help. In truth, I believe she always thought of me as a lazy boy, a useless extra, good only for building castles in the air, and was rather pleased to see me go. It was a dark night when I said goodbye. Taking along as assistants the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble, I carried the balloon, its basket, and its equipment with them by an indirect route to the place where we had stored the other supplies. Everything was still untouched, and I immediately set to work.

“It was the first of April. As I have said, the night was dark, without a single visible star. Drizzling rain fell now and then, making us very uncomfortable. My main worry, though, was the balloon. Despite its protective varnish, it was becoming heavy with moisture, and the powder could also be damaged. I therefore kept my three creditors hard at work, packing ice around the middle barrel and stirring the acid in the others. They constantly bothered me with questions about what I meant to do with all this equipment, and complained bitterly about the exhausting work. They could not see, they said, what good could come from getting soaked to the skin simply to take part in such dreadful spells. I grew anxious and worked with all my strength. I honestly believe the fools thought I had made a bargain with the devil and that my present activities were thoroughly wicked. I was therefore terribly afraid they would abandon me altogether. Still, I managed to calm them by promising to pay every debt in full as soon as I finished the business at hand. Naturally, they interpreted my promises in their own way. They doubtless imagined that, whatever else happened, I would acquire a vast amount of ready cash. Provided I paid everything I owed them, with a little extra for their work, I dare say they cared very little what became of either my soul or my body.

“After about four and a half hours, I found the balloon inflated enough. I attached the basket and loaded all my tools into it. I made sure to include the condensing equipment, a plentiful supply of water, and a large store of food, such as pemmican, which packs a great deal of nourishment into a fairly small amount of space."}

{"request_id":"795941397b932c1a400821dd","rewritten_text":"I come from a small land surrounded by sea and shaded by clouds. When I picture the whole globe, with its boundless ocean and unexplored continents, my homeland seems only a tiny speck in that immense whole. Yet in intellectual power it far outweighed countries with more land and more people. It is indeed the human mind alone that created everything good or great for humanity; Nature herself was only its chief servant. England, far to the north in the restless sea, now appears in my dreams as a vast ship with a full crew, mastering the winds and riding proudly over the waves. In my boyhood she was my entire universe. Standing on my native hills, I saw plains and mountains reach as far as my eyes could follow. My countrymen’s homes dotted them, and their labor had made the land fertile. To me, that spot was the very center of the earth. The rest of the world seemed like a story that neither my imagination nor my understanding would have struggled to forget.

From the beginning, my fortunes have shown how powerfully change can shape the course of a person’s life. In my case, this was almost an inheritance. Nature had showered my father with the enviable gifts of wit and imagination, then left these winds to drive his boat through life without reason to steer it or judgment to guide the voyage. He came from an obscure family, but circumstances brought him to public attention early. He soon spent the small property inherited from his father in the glittering world of fashion and luxury he had entered. During his brief, thoughtless youth, the fashionable idlers adored him. Among his greatest admirers was the young king, who escaped political intrigue and the difficult duties of ruling to find unfailing amusement and renewed spirits in my father’s company. My father could never control his impulses. They constantly led him into trouble from which only his ingenuity could rescue him. His growing mountain of gambling and business debts would have crushed anyone else, but he bore it cheerfully, with unstoppable good humor. His presence was so essential at the dinners and gatherings of the rich that they treated his failings as forgivable and welcomed him with praise that went to his head.

Popularity of this kind, like all popularity, soon fades. Meanwhile, the difficulties he faced in every direction increased at a frightening rate, far beyond his limited means of escaping them."}

{"request_id":"bb7e5b70ed047b83750f2dfb","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to hear that the beginning of this enterprise, which has filled you with such dark expectations, has brought no disaster. I arrived here yesterday, and my first duty is to assure my dear sister that I am well and increasingly confident of success.

I am already far north of London. Walking through the streets of Petersburg, I feel a cold northern breeze against my cheeks that steadies my nerves and fills me with delight. Can you understand that feeling? This breeze has traveled from the regions I am approaching and offers me a first taste of their icy climate. Encouraged by this promising wind, my daydreams grow more intense and vivid. I cannot persuade myself to picture the pole as a place of frost and desolation. In my imagination it remains a realm of beauty and pleasure. There, Margaret, the sun is always visible, its broad disk grazing the horizon and casting an unending splendor. There—for, with your permission, sister, I will place some trust in earlier navigators—snow and frost are banished. Sailing across a calm sea, we may be carried to a land whose wonders and beauty surpass every inhabited region yet discovered. Its natural life and landscape may be unique, as the behavior of the heavenly bodies undoubtedly is in those unexplored solitudes. What might we not expect in a country of eternal light? There I may discover the marvelous force that draws the compass needle, and bring order to a thousand astronomical observations whose apparent irregularities need only this voyage to become consistent forever. I will satisfy my burning curiosity by seeing a part of the world no one has visited, and may walk on ground no human foot has ever marked. These prospects draw me onward. They are enough to overcome every fear of danger or death and make me begin this demanding voyage with the delight of a child setting off in a little boat with holiday companions to explore his native river. But even if all these guesses prove false, you cannot deny the immeasurable benefit I will bring humanity, down to its final generation, by finding a passage near the pole to countries that currently take many months to reach. Or I may uncover the secret of magnetism, which, if it can be discovered at all, can only be revealed through an undertaking like mine.

These thoughts have driven away the anxiety with which I began this letter. My heart glows with an enthusiasm that lifts me to heaven, for nothing calms the mind so well as a firm purpose: a point on which the soul can fix its intellectual gaze."}

{"request_id":"0ca74bd5c6bb15dbfb6b19e7","rewritten_text":"Yet even then I had not drained the bitter cup to its last drop; I had not fully believed my loss; I did not yet know in every pulse, every nerve, every thought, that I alone survived of my race—that I was the LAST MAN.

Clouds had gathered over the day, and at sunset a fine rain began to fall. Even the everlasting heavens shed tears, I thought; why, then, should mortal man be ashamed to exhaust himself in weeping? I recalled the ancient tales in which human creatures melt through their tears into fountains that flow forever. Ah, if only it could be so! Then my fate would in some measure resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Oh, grief has strange fancies; it spins a web on which every shape and change around it inscribes the story of its sorrow. It mingles with all living nature and draws nourishment from every object; like light, it enters everything, and like light, lends everything its own hues.

My search had carried me some distance from the place where I had been cast ashore, until I reached one of the watchtowers standing at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I welcomed shelter, and welcomed a structure made by human hands after gazing so long upon nature’s desolate emptiness. Entering, I climbed the rough spiral stairs to the guardroom. Fate was kind this far: no distressing remnant of its former occupants remained. A few boards resting on two iron trestles, scattered with dried maize leaves, offered me a bed. An open chest holding some half-moldy biscuits stirred an appetite that perhaps had existed earlier, though I had not recognized it until now. A fierce, scorching thirst also tormented me, caused by the seawater I had swallowed and my body’s exhaustion. Kindly nature had attached pleasure to the satisfaction of these needs, so that I—even I!—grew refreshed and calmer while eating that wretched food and drinking a little sour wine from a half-filled flask left in the deserted dwelling. Then I lay down upon the bed, which no shipwrecked sufferer could scorn. After the loathsome smell of seaweed, the earthy scent of the dry leaves soothed my senses. I forgot my solitude. I looked neither behind me nor ahead; my senses settled into quiet rest. I slept and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, a shepherd whistling to summon his dog’s help in driving the flock into the fold, and the sights and sounds of my childhood among the mountains, which I had long since forgotten."}

{"request_id":"2aa0c827c2b400d64beec80d","rewritten_text":"“Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. Those responsible were corporeal beings and departed by corporeal means. How, then? Happily, there is only one way to reason on this question, and it must bring us to a definite conclusion. Let us consider the possible exits one at a time. Plainly, when the party came up the stairs, the assassins were either in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was discovered or in the adjoining chamber. Our search for outlets is therefore confined to those two rooms. The police have exposed the floors, ceilings, and wall masonry in every direction. No concealed passage could have eluded their scrutiny. Nevertheless, unwilling to rely upon their eyes, I inspected the place with my own. There were, indeed, no hidden exits. The two doors opening into the passage were firmly locked, their keys on the inside. Consider next the chimneys. Though of the usual width for eight or ten feet above the fireplaces, neither could accommodate a large cat along its entire length. Escape by the means already considered being absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. No one could have left through the front-room windows unseen by the people in the street. The murderers must consequently have used the windows of the rear chamber. Having reached this conclusion so unambiguously, we cannot, as reasoning men, dismiss it because it appears impossible. Our task is simply to demonstrate that these seeming ‘impossibilities’ are not, in fact, impossible.

“There are two windows in that room. Furniture obstructs neither the view nor the approach to one, which is entirely exposed. The lower part of the second is concealed by the head of the cumbersome bedstead pushed against it. The first was found fastened securely from inside and withstood the greatest efforts to lift it. A large hole had been bored with a gimlet into the left of its frame, and a particularly stout nail occupied it, driven nearly to its head. Inspection of the second window revealed a similar nail placed in precisely the same manner; energetic efforts to raise that sash were equally unsuccessful. The police felt completely assured that no escape had occurred in either direction. Removing the nails and opening the windows was therefore judged an unnecessary labor.

“My examination went rather further, for the reason I have just explained: I knew that it was here that every apparent impossibility must be shown to have no real existence.”"}

{"request_id":"9730dcd6162aeed1c535bf38","rewritten_text":"I passed several funeral processions, each with only a few mourners. People watching saw them as signs of terrible things to come. Some stared with a wild eagerness; others hurried away in fear; some cried aloud.

After arranging immediate help for the sick, Adrian’s main effort had been to hide the signs and spread of the plague from London’s people. He knew that fear and gloomy expectations helped disease take hold, and that despair and constant worry made the body especially vulnerable to infection. So no distressing sights were visible. Most shops stayed open, and there were still some people in the streets. Yet although London did not look like a plague-stricken city, it seemed greatly changed to me, since I had not seen it since the outbreak began. There were no carriages, and tall grass grew in the streets. The houses looked abandoned, with most shutters closed. The people I passed wore pale, frightened expressions, so unlike Londoners’ usual busy manner. My lone carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protector’s Palace. The fashionable streets around it looked even emptier and more depressing. I found Adrian’s waiting room crowded, for this was the hour when he received visitors. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited and watched people come and go. They were from the middle and lower classes, and had lost their livelihoods when trade and all the various forms of money-making so characteristic of our country had stopped. Those arriving looked anxious and sometimes terrified. Their faces were strikingly different from the calm, even satisfied expressions of those who had already seen him. In their livelier movements and happier faces I could see my friend’s influence. At two o’clock, no more people were admitted. Those who had missed their chance left in sadness or resentment, and I entered the room where Adrian received them.

I was struck by how much healthier he looked. He no longer drooped like a pampered spring flower that has grown beyond its strength and bends beneath the weight of its own blossoms. His eyes were bright, his face calm. His whole bearing showed a focused energy quite unlike his old weakness. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were sorting requests or recording the notes taken during that day’s meetings. Two or three people were still waiting for help. I admired his fairness and patience. Anyone who could support themselves outside London was advised to leave immediately, and he provided the means for them to do so."}

{"request_id":"3f4e75438abc41c62536f2bf","rewritten_text":"“Each compares it, not to someone speaking a national language he knows, but to precisely the reverse. The Frenchman believes the voice belonged to a Spaniard, and says he ‘might have made out words had he known Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it was a French voice; yet the report tells us that, ‘because he understood no French, the witness was questioned through an interpreter.’ The Englishman supposes the speaker German, although he ‘knows no German.’ The Spaniard ‘is certain’ it was an English voice, but bases his opinion entirely ‘on the intonation,’ since ‘he is unacquainted with English.’ The Italian takes it for a Russian voice, though he ‘has never spoken with a Russian native.’ Furthermore, another Frenchman contradicts the first, declaring positively that the voice was Italian; but as he does not know Italian, he too, like the Spaniard, ‘relies upon the intonation.’ How exceedingly strange that voice must have been to produce testimony of this description! Even its tones offered nothing recognizable to inhabitants of Europe’s five great divisions. You may suggest an Asiatic or an African speaker. Neither Asiatics nor Africans are numerous in Paris; without disputing your inference, however, I shall draw your attention to just three points. One witness describes the voice as ‘harsh rather than high-pitched.’ Two more characterize it as ‘rapid and uneven.’ Not one witness mentions distinguishing words, or even sounds that resembled words.

“I cannot tell,” Dupin went on, “what effect my reasoning has thus far had upon you. Nevertheless, I readily assert that sound deductions from this part of the evidence alone—the statements concerning the harsh and the shrill voices—suffice to create a suspicion that ought to guide every subsequent stage of the inquiry. I have said ‘sound deductions,’ but that does not quite convey my intention. I mean that these deductions are the only correct ones, and that this suspicion follows from them necessarily, as their sole outcome. I shall not yet disclose the suspicion itself. I ask you only to remember that it struck me powerfully enough to give my investigation of the room a particular shape and direction.

“Let us now imagine ourselves inside that chamber. What is the first thing we should look for? The route by which the murderers escaped. I think I may safely say that neither of us accepts supernatural occurrences.”"}

{"request_id":"346ee94e196f91ad841f3443","rewritten_text":"Each witness compares it to a speaker of a language he does not know, rather than one he understands. The Frenchman thinks it was a Spaniard's voice, and says he ‘might have recognized some words if he knew Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it belonged to a Frenchman. Yet we are told that, ‘because this witness does not understand French, he was questioned through an interpreter.’ The Englishman believes it was a German voice, though he ‘does not understand German.’ The Spaniard ‘is certain’ it was an Englishman, but judges entirely ‘by the tone,’ since ‘he knows no English.’ The Italian thinks it was a Russian, although he ‘has never spoken with anyone from Russia.’ A second Frenchman disagrees with the first and is sure the speaker was Italian. But he does not know Italian, and, like the Spaniard, bases his certainty on the tone. How extraordinarily strange that voice must have been to produce evidence like this! People from five major divisions of Europe could recognize nothing familiar even in its tones. You may suggest that it belonged to someone from Asia or Africa. There are few Asians or Africans in Paris, but without dismissing that possibility, I ask you to notice three things. One witness describes the voice as ‘harsh rather than high-pitched.’ Two others call it ‘rapid and uneven.’ None of the witnesses could identify any words, or even any sounds that resembled words.

“I do not know,” Dupin continued, “what you have made of my argument so far. But I can confidently say that sound reasoning from this part of the evidence alone—the testimony about the deep and high-pitched voices—is enough to raise a suspicion that should guide every further step in investigating the mystery. Even ‘sound reasoning’ does not fully express what I mean. These are the only conclusions that can properly be drawn, and they lead inevitably to one particular suspicion. I will not yet tell you what that suspicion is. For now, just remember that it was strong enough to give my examination of the room a definite purpose and direction.

“Let us now imagine that we are inside that room. What should we look for first? The way the murderers got out. I think I can safely say that neither of us believes in supernatural events.”"}

{"request_id":"9bc5d2b3b38e23090745ea3a","rewritten_text":"I removed its covering, and a light breeze soon stirred it into flame. Taking note of this as well, I fashioned a fan from branches, with which I revived the embers whenever they were almost dead. At the next coming of night I discovered with delight that the fire furnished light as well as warmth. This element proved useful also in preparing my food: some scraps abandoned by the travellers had been roasted, and I found them far more appetising than the berries I plucked from the trees. I therefore attempted to cook my own provisions similarly, laying them upon the glowing coals. This treatment ruined the berries, but greatly improved the nuts and roots.

“Food grew scarce, however, and I often devoted an entire day to seeking, unsuccessfully, a handful of acorns to quiet my hunger. Accordingly I determined to leave the spot where I had until then resided and find another where my few needs might be met more readily. In making this departure I deeply regretted losing the fire, which chance had given me and which I had no knowledge of creating anew. For several hours I earnestly considered this obstacle, but at last had to abandon every attempt to overcome it. Drawing my cloak about me, I set off through the wood in the direction of the sinking sun. Three days passed in such wanderings before I finally reached open country. Heavy snow had fallen during the previous night, leaving all the fields uniformly white. The prospect was cheerless, and the cold, damp material spread over the earth chilled my feet.

“It was roughly seven in the morning, and I yearned for something to eat and a place of refuge. Presently I discerned a little hut upon a rise, plainly erected for a shepherd's use. Never having beheld such a thing, I studied its construction with considerable curiosity. The door stood open, and I went inside. An old man sat by a fire, preparing his morning meal over it. Hearing a sound, he turned; at the sight of me he screamed, fled the hut, and raced over the fields at a pace his feeble frame would hardly have suggested. Both his appearance, unlike any I had previously encountered, and his flight astonished me somewhat. But the hut itself delighted me. Neither rain nor snow could enter; its floor was dry. To me it seemed as perfect and heavenly a sanctuary as Pandæmonium must have seemed to the demons of hell after their torments in the fiery lake.”"}

{"request_id":"b8da31c3ec75be5bc6959be1","rewritten_text":"It now showed my elevation to be 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles; thus at that moment I surveyed no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the earth’s entire surface. By nine o’clock the land to the east had again vanished from view, though not before I perceived that the balloon was drifting swiftly north-northwest. The curvature of the ocean below was strikingly clear, although drifting banks of cloud repeatedly obscured the prospect. I noticed that even the frailest vapors rose no higher than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I experimented by casting a handful of feathers out through the valve. Rather than floating as I had anticipated, they plunged straight downward together, like a bullet, with tremendous speed, and disappeared within a few seconds. At first I could find no explanation for this remarkable occurrence, since I could not believe my ascent had suddenly accelerated to such an extraordinary degree. Soon, however, it struck me that the air had become too thin to support even feathers. They had indeed fallen rapidly, just as they seemed to, and what astonished me was the combined speed of their descent and my rise.

“By ten I discovered that little required my immediate attention. Everything proceeded admirably, and I believed the balloon was ascending at a speed that increased every moment, though I could no longer measure the rate of that increase. I felt neither pain nor discomfort of any sort, and my spirits were better than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself alternately with inspecting my several instruments and renewing the atmosphere inside the chamber. I resolved to perform this latter task regularly every forty minutes, less because such frequent renewal was strictly necessary than to safeguard my health. Meanwhile I could not resist imagining what lay ahead. My fancy delighted in the moon’s wild, dreamlike regions. Imagination, freed for once from her restraints, wandered wherever she pleased through the shifting marvels of an indistinct and changeable land. At one moment there were ancient, venerable forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing noisily into bottomless chasms. At the next I found myself abruptly in hushed noonday wildernesses, where no breath of wind ever entered, and where immense fields of poppies and slender flowers resembling lilies stretched to a weary distance, forever motionless and silent."}

{"request_id":"9a8f7703ac41640d96fbb07b","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and hopeless. Her usual activities no longer pleased her. Any pleasure seemed disrespectful to the dead, and she believed that endless grief and tears were the only proper tribute to innocence so cruelly destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who had walked beside the lake with me when we were younger, speaking eagerly of our future. She had suffered the first of those sorrows that loosen our attachment to life, and its shadow had put out her sweetest smiles.

“When I think, my dear cousin,” she said, “of Justine Moritz's terrible death, the world and everything in it look different to me. Before this, the stories of cruelty and injustice I read in books or heard from others seemed to belong to the distant past, or to describe imaginary evils. At least, they were far away: I understood them in my mind without truly picturing them. Now suffering has reached our home, and people seem like monsters hungry for each other's blood. Yet surely I am unfair. Everyone thought the poor girl was guilty. If she had committed the crime for which she died, she would indeed have been among the most wicked people alive. To kill the son of her friend and benefactor for a few jewels—a child she had cared for since birth and seemed to love as her own! I could never agree to anyone's execution, but I would certainly have thought such a person unfit to live among others. Yet she was innocent. I know it; I feel it. You believe it too, and that strengthens my certainty. Oh, Victor, when a lie can look so much like truth, who can feel sure of lasting happiness? I feel as though I am walking along a cliff's edge, with thousands of people pressing toward me, trying to push me into the depths below. William and Justine were murdered, and the killer goes unpunished. He walks freely through the world, perhaps even respected. But even if I were condemned to die on the scaffold for those same crimes, I would never exchange places with that wretched man.”

Listening to her caused me unbearable pain. Though I had not committed the acts myself, I was in effect the real murderer. Elizabeth saw my anguish in my face. She gently took my hand and said, “My dearest friend, you must try to be calm. God knows how deeply these events have hurt me, but I am not as miserable as you are.”"}

{"request_id":"e5920e86c98411652037b7f4","rewritten_text":"Spirits of the storm, receive me! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever! Unless a kinder power has chosen another end, so that after long suffering I may receive my reward and feel my heart beating close to another heart like mine again.

The Tiber, a road laid by nature's own hand through her continent, lay at my feet, and many boats were tied along its banks. With a few books, some provisions, and my dog, I would take one of them and drift down the river to the sea. Then, staying close to land, I would follow the beautiful shores and sunny headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and travel along Calabria, and brave the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis. Fearlessly—for what had I to lose?—I would cross the sea toward Malta and the more distant Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople: its familiar towers and inlets belonged to a life utterly different from mine now. I would follow Asia Minor and Syria, pass the Nile's seven mouths, and turn north again. Leaving forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya behind, I would reach the pillars of Hercules. And then—wherever I might go—the muddy caves and silent depths of the sea might become my home before this long voyage ended. Disease might strike my heart as I drifted alone on the restless Mediterranean. Or, somewhere I landed, I might find what I sought—a companion. If that could not be, the solitary wanderer would keep raising his sail and gripping the tiller forever, frail and grey-haired, his youth already buried with those he loved. Still following heaven's breezes, he would round one headland after another, anchor in bay after bay, and cross the barren ocean. Leaving the green land of his native Europe behind, I might sail down Africa's tawny coast, survive the Cape's fierce seas, and moor my battered little boat in a creek shaded by fragrant spice groves on distant islands of the Indian Ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet they have ruled my imagination ever since they came to me a week ago, as I stood high on St. Peter's. I have chosen my boat and put my few supplies aboard. I have selected a handful of books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare. But the world's libraries stand open to me, and I can replenish my collection in any port. I do not expect any change for the better; yet I cannot endure the sameness of the present."}

{"request_id":"eeeee0757b17ac062bc61a9f","rewritten_text":"My father had been the life of my life, and his memory was so still. I could feel gratitude toward someone else, but I could never again love or hope as I once had. Everything was suffering; even my pleasures were endured, not enjoyed. I was like a lonely place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black cliffs, where no warmth could enter and no path led out to sunnier fields. So, although friendship soothed me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitor; it left, and I scarcely felt its loss. The will to live was dead within me. Do not be surprised, then, that I welcomed heaven's best gift—a friend—with so little joy, or mourned its departure with so little bitterness.

My friend's name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his story, so that you may judge how cold my heart must have been to resist his eloquent words and tender sympathy. You will also see how, since he too was deeply unhappy, we might have comforted each other, had misery not turned me to stone like the head of Medusa. Woodville's troubles did not strike at the heart's deepest core as mine did. His grief was natural, capable of purifying the heart rather than destroying it. When its shadow passed, he might emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had been educated in the classics. He was among those very few people whom fortune favours from birth, showering them without limit with gifts of mind and body. Under her special protection, no imperfection, however slight, and no disappointment, however brief, seemed permitted to touch him. She appeared to have made his mind from excellence that nothing base could stain, and given him an understanding that no error could lead astray. His genius was extraordinary; when it rose like a bright star in the east, everyone turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet. That name has been cheapened so often that it cannot convey everything he was. He resembled an ancient poet whom the Muses had crowned in his cradle and whose lips had nourished bees. As he walked among other men, a heavenly glow seemed to surround him, setting him apart and raising him above them. His exceptional beauty, the dazzling fire in his eyes, and the rich sound of his words, which left listeners silent with delighted wonder, made him tower above everyone else. In his presence, they seemed to have been created only to serve his greater excellence."}

{"request_id":"51b58cfa58a75de7bc310aee","rewritten_text":"“I then reasoned backward from the evidence. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If so, they could not have fastened the windows from inside afterward, as they were found fastened. This apparently obvious objection had brought the police investigation of the windows to a halt. Yet they were fastened. They must therefore be capable of fastening themselves. There was no avoiding that conclusion. I went to the unobstructed window, pulled out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to lift the sash. As I had expected, it resisted every effort. I now knew there must be a concealed spring, and this confirmation convinced me that my initial reasoning, at least, was sound, however mysterious the nails remained. A careful search soon revealed the hidden spring. I pressed it and, satisfied with what I had discovered, left the sash down.

“I then put the nail back and examined it closely. Anyone climbing out through this window could have shut it afterward, allowing the spring to catch, but could not have replaced the nail. The conclusion was clear and narrowed my investigation further. The murderers must have escaped through the other window. Assuming the springs on both windows were alike, as seemed probable, there must be some difference between the nails, or at least in the way they were fitted. Climbing onto the bed's fabric support, I examined the second window closely over the headboard. I slid my hand down behind the board and easily found and pressed the spring. As I had supposed, it worked exactly like the other. I then inspected the nail. It was just as thick as its counterpart and apparently fitted in the same way, driven in almost to its head.

“You will say I was baffled; but if you think that, you have misunderstood the reasoning. To borrow a phrase from hunting, I had never once lost the trail. Not for an instant had I lost the scent. Every link in the chain was sound. I had followed the mystery to its final point, and that point was the nail. As I say, in every respect it looked identical to the nail in the other window. But however conclusive that resemblance might appear, it counted for absolutely nothing beside the fact that the trail ended here, at precisely this spot. ‘There must be something wrong with the nail,’ I told myself. I touched it, and the head, together with about a quarter of an inch of the shaft, came away in my fingers."}

{"request_id":"bb3e88bbbd2f5271586f72e0","rewritten_text":"‘I then reasoned backward from the facts. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If that was true, they could not have fastened the windows again from inside, as we found them. This obvious difficulty had stopped the police from investigating further in that direction. Yet the windows were fastened. They must therefore be able to fasten themselves. There was no avoiding that conclusion. I went to the window that was not blocked, pulled out its nail with some difficulty, and tried to lift the sliding frame. It would not move, however hard I tried, just as I had expected. Now I knew there must be a hidden spring. This confirmation persuaded me that at least my starting assumptions were right, although the nails remained a mystery. A careful search soon revealed the spring. I pressed it and, satisfied with my discovery, left the window closed.

‘I then put the nail back and studied it closely. Someone climbing out through this window could have closed it behind them, allowing the spring to catch. But they could not have replaced the nail. The conclusion was clear, and once again it narrowed my search. The murderers must have escaped through the other window. If both frames had the same springs, as seemed likely, then there must be a difference between the nails, or at least in how they were fitted. I climbed onto the bed's canvas support and examined the second window closely over the headboard. Reaching down behind the board, I easily found and pressed the spring. As I had expected, it was just like the other one. Next I examined the nail. It was equally thick and seemed to be fitted in exactly the same way, driven in almost to its head.

‘You may say I must have been puzzled. If so, you have misunderstood my reasoning. To borrow a term from hunting, I had never once lost the trail. Not for an instant had the scent disappeared. Every link in the chain of reasoning was sound. I had followed the secret to its final point, and that point was the nail. As I have said, it looked in every way like the nail in the other window. But that fact, convincing though it might seem, counted for nothing beside the knowledge that the trail ended here, at this very spot. “There must be something wrong with the nail,” I said. I touched it, and the head, with about a quarter of an inch of the shaft, came away in my fingers.’"}

{"request_id":"df873da2b10f21d0e9e19665","rewritten_text":"The following day he remained indoors, spending, in fact, nearly all his time in his own room, sick with a frantic dread of death and yet unconcerned with life itself. The sense of being pursued, trapped, hunted down was beginning to possess him. A mere stirring of the tapestry in the wind made him tremble. Dead leaves blowing against the leaded windows seemed like his own squandered resolutions and desperate regrets. Whenever he shut his eyes, the sailor's face reappeared, peering through the mist-clouded glass, and horror seemed to press its hand against his heart again.

Yet perhaps only his fancy had summoned vengeance from the darkness and placed those dreadful shapes of punishment before him. Real life was confusion, but imagination possessed a terrible logic. Imagination made remorse follow at the heels of sin; imagination gave every crime its deformed offspring. In the ordinary realm of facts, evil went unpunished and goodness unrewarded. The strong were granted success, the weak burdened with failure. That was everything. Besides, if a stranger had been skulking about the house, surely a servant or keeper would have noticed him. If footprints had appeared in the flowerbeds, the gardeners would have mentioned them. Yes, it was nothing but fancy. Sibyl Vane's brother had not returned to murder him. He had gone away aboard his ship, to perish in some wintry sea. From that man, at least, he was secure. Why, the fellow did not know his identity, could not possibly know it. His youthful mask had rescued him.

And still, if it had been no more than an illusion, how dreadful to reflect that conscience could summon such terrifying spectres, clothe them in visible shapes, and set them moving before one's eyes! What kind of existence awaited him if, night and day, the shadows of his crime watched from quiet corners, jeered from hidden places, whispered into his ear while he sat at a feast, or roused him with frozen fingers in his sleep? As this thought stole through his mind, terror drained his face, and the air seemed suddenly to grow colder. Oh, in what a frenzied hour of madness had he murdered his friend! How hideous even the recollection of that scene! It all unfolded before him once more. Every dreadful detail returned with a fresh intensity of horror. From time's black cavern arose the image of his sin, terrible and wrapped in scarlet."}

{"request_id":"25206fa36999ea2c58b91225","rewritten_text":"Throughout his present brief visit, Emma had seen scarcely anything of him, though enough to feel that the first encounter was behind her, and to conclude that the blend of resentment and self-importance now marking his manner was no improvement. Indeed, she was beginning to marvel that she had ever found him agreeable. The sight of him was bound so closely to unpleasant sensations that, save as a moral exercise—a penance, a lesson, a salutary humiliation of her pride—she would gladly have learned that she need never see him again. She wished him every happiness; but he caused her pain, and his prosperity at twenty miles’ distance would have pleased her best.

His marriage, however, must surely soften the discomfort of his continuing to live in Highbury. It would forestall many needless anxieties and ease many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would justify any alteration in their intercourse; their old familiarity could diminish unnoticed. Their civil relations would almost begin afresh.

Emma gave little thought to the lady herself. Doubtless she was sufficiently good for Mr. Elton, sufficiently accomplished for Highbury, sufficiently handsome—probably to appear plain beside Harriet. Concerning her connections Emma felt entirely secure, convinced that, after boasting of his own claims and scorning Harriet’s, he had gained nothing. On this subject the truth seemed discoverable. What sort of person she was remained uncertain, but who she was could be established; and apart from the ten thousand pounds, she seemed in no respect Harriet’s superior. She contributed neither a distinguished name nor ancestry nor alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, as of course one must call him. Yet the whole proceeds of his commercial career appeared so modest that one might fairly suppose the dignity of his trade equally modest. She had customarily spent part of each winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home, the very centre of Bristol. Although her parents had died some years earlier, an uncle survived—in the law. No more precisely honourable description had been ventured than that he was in the law, and the daughter had lived with him. Emma imagined him some attorney’s overworked subordinate, too dull to advance. The entire magnificence of the connection seemed to rest upon the elder sister, who had married very well indeed: a gentleman living in great style near Bristol, who maintained two carriages! There the account concluded; there lay Miss Hawkins’ glory."}

{"request_id":"969957fe71e40d3839707a33","rewritten_text":"Yet even then I had not swallowed the last of that bitter draught. I was not fully convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel in every heartbeat, every nerve, every thought, that I alone remained of my race—that I was the last man.

Clouds had gathered during the day, and at sunset a fine rain began to fall. Even the everlasting heavens weep, I thought; why should a mortal man be ashamed to exhaust himself in tears? I recalled the ancient stories in which human beings dissolve through weeping into fountains that flow forever. If only it could be so! Then my fate would share something with the drowning deaths of Adrian and Clara. How strange grief is! From every shape and change around it, grief weaves a fabric on which to record its sorrow. It merges with all living nature and draws nourishment from every object. Like light, it enters everything; like light, it gives everything its own colours.

My search had taken me some distance from the place where I had washed ashore, and I reached one of the watchtowers spaced along the Italian coast. I welcomed the shelter, glad to discover something made by human hands after gazing so long at nature’s desolate emptiness. I entered and climbed the rough spiral staircase to the guardroom. Fate was merciful enough to have left no distressing trace of its former occupants. A few boards resting across two iron trestles and scattered with dried corn leaves offered me a bed. An open chest containing some half-mouldy biscuits awakened an appetite that perhaps had already existed, though I had not noticed it until now. I was also tormented by fierce, parching thirst, caused by the seawater I had swallowed and my body’s exhaustion. Kindly nature had made the satisfaction of these needs a source of pleasure, so that I—even I!—felt refreshed and soothed as I ate this wretched food and drank a little sour wine from a half-full flask left in the deserted tower. Then I lay down on the bed, which a shipwrecked man had no reason to despise. After the hateful smell of seaweed, the earthy scent of the dried leaves comforted my senses. I forgot my loneliness. I looked neither into the past nor toward the future; my senses settled into quiet. I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, the shepherd whistling for his dog to help drive the flock into its pen, and the sights and sounds of my mountain childhood that I had long forgotten."}

{"request_id":"ccbc7cb0c407a1017f8138fd","rewritten_text":"Once this was done, I detached a few more loops from the rim, worked in another section of cloth, and fastened the loose loops to their corresponding buttons. In this way I could insert the entire upper portion of the bag between the netting and the hoop. Clearly the hoop would now fall inside the basket, leaving the buttons alone to support the full weight of the basket and everything it contained. At first this might seem an inadequate support, but it was nothing of the kind. The buttons were exceptionally strong and placed so closely together that each bore only a very small fraction of the total load. Indeed, I would have felt no concern even if the basket and its contents had weighed three times as much. I then lifted the hoop again inside the rubber covering and supported it at almost its previous height with three lightweight poles prepared for this purpose. Naturally, this kept the top of the bag spread open and held the lower part of the netting in its proper position. All that remained was to seal the opening of the enclosure. I easily accomplished this by gathering the folds of material together and twisting them tightly from inside with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“Three circular panes of thick, clear glass had been fitted into the sides of the covering now surrounding the basket. These allowed me an unobstructed view in every horizontal direction. A fourth window of the same kind was set into the fabric beneath me, aligned with a small opening in the basket's floor. Through it I could look straight down. However, I had been unable to fit any similar arrangement overhead because of the particular way the upper opening was closed and the resulting folds in the cloth. I therefore could not expect to see anything directly above me. This scarcely mattered, of course: even if I had managed to install a window at the top, the balloon itself would have blocked the view and made it useless.

“About a foot beneath one of the side windows was a circular opening eight inches across. It was fitted with a brass rim whose inner edge was threaded to receive a screw. Into this rim I screwed the condenser's large tube, while the body of the machine remained, of course, inside the rubber chamber."}
